{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789344071,"nonce":"11HkMzEJWrT6BEeVUvxSSnI88AA=","message":"Construct, and what was actually costing it.\n\nNothing in the earlier rounds touched construct; it sat at 268–305 (Haxe→Wren) and 285–305 (Wren→Haxe) alone, and the readings above 350 and the 663/790 in a full-table run were the machine under an XProtect scan and another session's test suites (the full table also shares one heap across cells, which inflates every allocating cell). The Haxe→Wren closure's 80–95 readings were the same load; back to back at low load it is 54–55, where it was.\n\nWhere a Haxe→Wren construct's ~305 went (macOS `sample` tree over the loop): ~40% inside collections fired by the allocation — the sweep's drop hooks removing every dead ref from the process-wide `REFS` map, cache-cold, and the mark waiting on the pool — plus the map's mutex three times per object and its insert. An experiment build that skipped the map altogether ran the cell at 130 against 304 interleaved. The same disease on the other side: `finalize_dead` did a hash lookup in the `live` map for every dead instance, whether or not it was a proxy, so every Wren program under caribou paid it.\n\nFix (this commit): the object keeps its own stand-in. The protocol gains `shadow`, `keep_shadow` and `drop_shadow`, optional: the object of another language standing for this one, kept by the object's language. caribou-wren keeps it in the prefix's second word, which no longer has to hold the record: word zero is now the descriptor the VM's heap record carries (`WrenHeap` is `repr(C)` with its `TypeDesc` first, and a record is never freed, so a value that outlives its VM still reads a descriptor and is refused by the VM check). The word holds the shadow above three flag bits, `MARKED`, `PLAIN` and the new `ADOPTED`; the cycle writes the flags plainly under the GC lock it holds throughout, `keep_shadow` takes the lock, `drop_shadow` runs under it from the ref's drop hook, and the rare flag writers (`watch`, `set_adopted`) are atomic RMWs. caribou-ash's `wrap_foreign`/`foreign_ref`/`drop_ref` go through the shadow, and keep the `REFS` map only for an object whose language answers `Unsupported` (a core `Error`). The `live` map is gone: `adopt` sets `ADOPTED`, the sweep releases the handle it reads from the instance's field only for those, and `heap_drop` walks the pins for them. No memory grows: the prefix is still sixteen bytes.\n\nEach cell alone, ns, at a load of ~8: construct Wren→Wren 33 → 28.7, Haxe→Wren 305 → 135–140, Wren→Haxe 285 → 138–145. Unchanged: Haxe→Haxe 6.5.\n\nWhat is left in the ~138: the mark phase waiting on the pool (~14%; the core traces every Wren object each collection, by the anchor's pins), one thread-local read per allocation in the core's TLAB path (~13%, three allocations per construct plus a deferred release), zeroing (~9%), the ref's locked allocation and `handle_new` (~5%), wren_lift's own `call_constructor_sync`, which hashes a string per construct (~9%, also in the Wren→Wren 28.7), and the `Dynamic` boxing of the constructor argument, since `construct new(t)` in the bench declares no `#export`.\n\nThe Haxe→Wren closure (54) is not a bridge cost at all: `Fn` exports as `Dynamic`, so Haxe's call boxes each argument and `hlp_dyn_call` packs a var-args array, two allocations per call before the bridge sees it. A typed `Fn(Num) -\u003e Num` export needs a typed closure whose entry Ash calls by record, which is an Ash feature (a closure over a host native by record); until then that cell measures `Reflect.makeVarArgs`.","files":null}]}